// Three support tickets straight out of a CRM. `id` is YOUR record id:
// it is not personal data, it is never sent to Synthaveil, and it is the only
// thing you need to put the model's answer back on the right row.
//
// Note what `body` does NOT contain: a name. Free text is where personal data
// hides, and tokenising the `name` and `email` columns does nothing for it —
// an earlier version of this example named the customer in `body` while
// claiming the model never saw them, which was simply untrue.
// If YOUR free text mentions people, protect it too: run it through the Scan
// operation, or map it to an L3 free-text method, before it reaches a model.
return [
  { json: { id: 'T-4821', name: 'Jane Testerson', email: 'jane.testerson@example.com', body: 'Customer called about their invoice being wrong again.' } },
  { json: { id: 'T-4822', name: 'Ahmed Farouk',   email: 'ahmed.farouk@example.com',   body: 'Customer asked to move their renewal date.' } },
  { json: { id: 'T-4823', name: 'Priya Nair',     email: 'priya.nair@example.com',     body: 'Customer reported the export button doing nothing.' } },
];

// Stand-in for your AI step (OpenAI, Anthropic, a local model — whichever).
// Swap this node for the real one; the only thing that matters downstream is
// that the model's output still carries `id`.
//
// Note what the model receives: a synthetic name and email, the ticket id, and
// a `body` that names nobody. It never sees the real customers. That last part
// is only true because `body` was written without names in it — protecting the
// name/email columns alone would not have made it true.
return items.map((item) => ({
  json: {
    id: item.json.id,
    summary: `Summary for ${item.json.name}: ${String(item.json.body).slice(0, 40)}…`,
  },
}));
